e the  job of the Jubilee USA network is really  to try to provide the knowledge and  tools for people of faith to you know to  bring that human face to public  discourse and to make sure that we're  not forgetting that and and so is the  comments I would like to make is just  really that that there is the power of  the faith community to make change in  public policy and in complicated  economic policy that is about economic  injustice and about fighting poverty and  as many probably know the story of the  Jubilee movement is is that that there  were people that were willing to  challenge of the ekit the economic  paradigms and say you know you're being  you're telling us that loan contracts  between with these governments that  those are sacred but really what sacred  is human dignity and our sacred texts  tell us that it is time for us to to  engage in debt cancellation so that  these resources can be invested in  health care education and so forth in in  a way that the changes some of the power  dynamics and that we're not just talking  about a charity model and and at the  time we were told it couldn't be done  and it's a story of churches and people  in the pews and institutional leaders  standing up and saying no we can change  this public policy and actually have  made really important gains a hundred  billion dollars have in debt stock has  been cancelled 32 countries have  received debt cancellation and that has  translated into concrete investments on  the ground in many of these countries so  I think that that's a story that we need  to be bringing and it's an unfinished  agenda and actually the fact that the  senator spoke about Kenya Kenya actually  was left out of those deals so there are  countries that have been that that need  continued debt cancellation and then  countries that fall into crisis like in  Haiti where we need to do that but in  terms of  but I really think that it's reflecting  on Lent and on the idea of sacrifice in  Lent I think wh